# AgriJobHub Malawi

A full-stack job board for agriculture, economics, agribusiness, and business
administration roles in Malawi — Node/Express backend with a JSON REST API,
and a vanilla HTML/CSS/JS frontend.

## What's included

```
agrijobhub-app/
├── server.js          Express server + REST API
├── package.json        Dependencies and scripts
├── data/
│   ├── jobs.json        Seed job listings (Malawi companies & locations)
│   ├── companies.json   Featured employer profiles
│   ├── resources.json   Article/resource cards
│   └── alerts.json       Created automatically when someone subscribes
└── public/
    ├── index.html        Page markup
    ├── styles.css         All styling
    └── app.js             Frontend logic (fetches from the API)
```

## Requirements

- [Node.js](https://nodejs.org) version 18 or later (includes npm)

## Setup

1. Unzip the project and open a terminal in the `agrijobhub-app` folder.
2. Install dependencies:
   ```
   npm install
   ```
3. Start the server:
   ```
   npm start
   ```
4. Open **http://localhost:3000** in your browser.

For auto-restart on file changes during development, use `npm run dev`
instead (this uses `nodemon`, listed as a dev dependency).

## API endpoints

| Method | Path              | Description                                   |
|--------|-------------------|------------------------------------------------|
| GET    | `/api/jobs`        | List jobs. Query params: `q`, `category`, `location`, `type`, `sort` (`recent`, `salary-high`, `salary-low`) |
| GET    | `/api/jobs/:id`     | Get a single job by id                        |
| POST   | `/api/jobs`         | Create a new job listing (JSON body)          |
| GET    | `/api/locations`    | Distinct list of job locations                |
| GET    | `/api/companies`    | Featured employer profiles                    |
| GET    | `/api/resources`    | Article/resource cards                        |
| POST   | `/api/alerts`       | Subscribe an email to job alerts              |

Job data lives in `data/jobs.json` and is read/written by the server, so
listings posted through the "Post a job" button on the site persist between
restarts. Favorited jobs are stored in the visitor's browser (`localStorage`),
not on the server.

## About the seed data

The included jobs and companies (Lilongwe, Blantyre, Mzuzu, Zomba, Kasungu,
Dwangwa; employers such as FAO Malawi, Illovo Sugar Malawi, Pyxus Agriculture
Malawi, the Reserve Bank of Malawi, and others) reflect the kinds of roles
and organizations that regularly recruit in Malawi's agriculture, economics,
agribusiness, and business administration sectors, based on research done at
the time this project was built. They are illustrative starting data, not a
live, auto-updating feed — there's no continuously syncing job-board API
wired in. To keep listings current, either:

- Edit `data/jobs.json` directly (or use the "Post a job" form), or
- Wire up `server.js` to fetch from a real job-board API or scraper you have
  access to (a scheduled fetch that rewrites `jobs.json` would be the
  simplest way to do this) — swap the `readJSON`/`writeJSON` calls in
  `server.js` for a database of your choice if you outgrow the JSON files.

## Deploying to Vercel

This project includes a `vercel.json` so it can run as a Vercel serverless
function:

1. Push this folder to a GitHub repo (or run `vercel` from inside it with the
   [Vercel CLI](https://vercel.com/docs/cli)).
2. Import the repo in the Vercel dashboard, or run `vercel deploy`.
3. No environment variables are required.

**Important tradeoff:** Vercel's filesystem is read-only at runtime except
for `/tmp`, which is wiped on every cold start and redeploy. This project
detects `process.env.VERCEL` and writes posted jobs / alert emails to `/tmp`
instead of `data/`, so it won't crash — but anything posted through the site
will eventually disappear rather than persisting permanently. For real
persistence in production, swap the `readJSON`/`writeJSON` calls in
`server.js` for a database (Vercel Postgres, Supabase, MongoDB Atlas, etc.).

If you don't need the backend features (posting jobs, email alerts) to
persist across visitors at all, the single self-contained HTML file version
is simpler to deploy to Vercel/Netlify and never has this issue, since it
has no server component.

## Notes

- No API keys or external services are required to run this out of the box.
- Currency is shown in Malawian Kwacha (MWK), monthly.
- The "Apply now" and "Post a job" actions are functional UI flows for this
  prototype; they don't yet send real emails or route applications anywhere.
